    "topic_id": 2815107,
    "poster_id": 577109,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/b/46ba6ed097cc88ded349bf99eea89817f135f470.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"34\" height=\"11\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e590e42638402f8f07008a4a9fda9a1a675ec6dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1,a_2,\\dots,a_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"103\" height=\"11\" > be arbitrary positive integers. Ali and Mohammad Play the following game. At each step, Ali chooses <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9867f312f29e5702144a39f36976b926c2c32b15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_1,b_2,\\dots,b_m \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"136\" height=\"18\" > and then Mohammad chosses a positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\"  width=\"8\" height=\"8\" > and obtains a new sequence <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/d/5ddcc5c2d5c1ea9ebe365e47aa37a8fb4987d53c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{c_i=a_i+b_{i+s}\\}_{i=1}^m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"147\" height=\"20\" >,</span> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/e/b0eec178369d9c8bc0793010882781b4ae08663c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"$$b_{m+1}=b_1,\\ b_{m+2}=b_2, \\dots,\\ b_{m+s}=b_s$$\"  width=\"287\" height=\"17\" >The goal of Ali is to make all the numbers divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > in a finite number of steps. FInd all positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > such that Ali has a winning strategy, no matter how the initial values <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/3/cc377c6799eb98a5358df1a5ba05a2fc2d2e86ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2,\\dots,a_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"107\" height=\"13\" > are.<br>\n<span class=\"cmty-hide-heading faux-link\" onclick=\"AoPS.Community.Utils.clickHide($(this));\" href=\"#\">clarification</span><div class=\"cmty-hide-content\" style=\"display:none\">after we create the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/9/1e95be43c04d6869c67861aae1e3e69123fedd8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" > s, this sequence becomes the sequence that we continue playing on, as in it is our 'new' <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/2/412787c048e28774dc63fc27db42dc52ca858de7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" ></div><br>\n<br>\nProposed by Shayan Gholami",
    "post_canonical": "Let $m,n$ and $a_1,a_2,\\dots,a_m$ be arbitrary positive integers. Ali and Mohammad Play the following game. At each step, Ali chooses $b_1,b_2,\\dots,b_m \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and then Mohammad chosses a positive integers $s$ and obtains a new sequence $\\{c_i=a_i+b_{i+s}\\}_{i=1}^m$, where $$b_{m+1}=b_1,\\ b_{m+2}=b_2, \\dots,\\ b_{m+s}=b_s$$ The goal of Ali is to make all the numbers divisible by $n$ in a finite number of steps. FInd all positive integers $m$ and $n$ such that Ali has a winning strategy, no matter how the initial values $a_1, a_2,\\dots,a_m$ are.\n[hide=clarification] after we create the $c_i$ s, this sequence becomes the sequence that we continue playing on, as in it is our 'new' $a_i$[/hide]\n\nProposed by Shayan Gholami",
