    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 2815113,
    "poster_id": 577109,
    "post_rendered": "We call an infinite set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/4/554eb9b4ccbfde135f88d24219444ab6bf430c7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"51\" height=\"17\" > good if for all parwise different integers <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/6/116a2aae2204d9bb4426eab69a7ed1171e8a02cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,c\\in S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" >,</span> all positive divisors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/a/25ab236187e19f59066353a78177ef31a9626c2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{a^c-b^c}{a-b}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"59\" height=\"41\" > are in <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\"  width=\"12\" height=\"12\" >.</span> for all positive integers <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/65262bf247c4a3c6114a8223d249dc9702619d3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" >,</span> prove that there exists a good set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\"  width=\"12\" height=\"12\" > such that <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/5/015962b724aeeb23f0debf213a3a00acbef2e39f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\not \\in S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"47\" height=\"18\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\nProposed by Seyed Reza Hosseini Dolatabadi",
    "post_canonical": "We call an infinite set $S\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}$ good if for all parwise different integers $a,b,c\\in S$, all positive divisors of $\\frac{a^c-b^c}{a-b}$ are in $S$. for all positive integers $n>1$, prove that there exists a good set $S$ such that $n \\not \\in S$.\n\nProposed by Seyed Reza Hosseini Dolatabadi",
